{
  "$comment": "Language-neutral drift fixture for ARCHETYPE EXPANSION — the ONE pin holding the two implementations together: aidream/services/content_plan/archetypes.py (CANONICAL, Python: parse_archetype_map + expand_archetype) and matrx-frontend/features/marketing/content-plan/setup/archetypes.ts (browser twin, TS). Both must run every case identically, because the frontend previews and writes plan.node rows locally while the chat tool writes them server-side — a divergence means the preview a human approved is not what lands. `nodes` is the depth-first walk of the expanded tree in WRITE order (parents before children); it deliberately pins slug/node_type/page_type/brief/attributes rather than route, because the Python spec model carries no route (routes are pinned separately in `routes`, which BOTH sides compute the same way plan._node_shape does). `foundation` order is significant: tokens, header, footer, nav_entries, then assets in declaration order; items resolving to 0 are DROPPED. `declared_as` keeps the raw declaration so a UI can show that 2 came from \"=services.count\". Precedence pinned by cases 2-3: `names` overrides the archetype default count, an explicit `counts` entry overrides both, and a supplied name list longer than the count is truncated. error_cases pin that malformed config is LOUD in both languages (a half-parsed archetype silently drops the counts that are the whole point); they assert substrings that both implementations genuinely contain — never exact message text, which is language-specific. Extra/unknown config keys are REJECTED on both sides (Python: model_config extra='forbid').",
